<main id="main" class="maps-alternative" data-rangefind-body>
  <section class="section public-index">
    <span class="eyebrow">PLANET-SCALE OSM INDEX · READY TO QUERY</span>
    <h2>Use the free public OpenStreetMap index now</h2>
    <p>
      You do not have to build a planet index to try Rangefind or ship an
      integration. <a href="https://osm.rangefind.dev/">osm.rangefind.dev</a>
      is a ready-generated, cross-origin public index that works directly from
      browsers and Node. It needs no Rangefind account, API key, or payment
      method.
    </p>
    <div class="quickstart public-index__grid">
      <div>
        <h3>Connect in three lines</h3>
        <pre><code class="language-js">import { createSearch } from "rangefind";

const engine = await createSearch({
  baseUrl: "https://osm.rangefind.dev/"
});</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Free public service, or fully self-hosted</h3>
        <p>
          The public index is free to query and continuously refreshed on a
          best-effort basis. Use it for prototypes, public apps, evaluation,
          and integrations that can tolerate a community service without an
          availability SLA. Mirror or build the index under your own domain
          when you need version pinning, guaranteed capacity, custom fields,
          or an operational SLA.
        </p>
        <p>
          Display
          <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright"><strong>© OpenStreetMap contributors</strong></a>
          anywhere results or map data are shown.
          <a href="https://osm.rangefind.dev/">Check coverage and freshness →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <span class="eyebrow">REPLACE THE REQUEST PATH, NOT THE WHOLE MAP</span>
    <h2>Where Rangefind replaces Google Maps APIs</h2>
    <p>
      Rangefind is an open-source alternative for applications that need
      search and geocoding over OpenStreetMap data without sending every query
      to a paid provider. It is intentionally not a clone of the entire Google
      Maps Platform.
    </p>
    <div class="features maps-capabilities">
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Places and autocomplete</h3>
        <p>Search names, brands, categories, localities, streets, civic addresses, and multilingual aliases as the user types.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Forward and reverse geocoding</h3>
        <p>Resolve explicit and interpolated addresses, or turn a map click into nearby addresses, streets, and localities.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Nearby and viewport search</h3>
        <p>Use exact radius, nearest-first, bounding-box, open-now, accessibility, service, and payment constraints.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Routes and turn-by-turn steps</h3>
        <p>Car, bike, and foot routes with exact turn restrictions and steps that carry what the signs say — road number, exit number, roundabout exit, posted limit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Matrices and multi-stop itineraries</h3>
        <p>All-pairs travel times from one shared search, and stop ordering that is exact to 10 interior stops — Compute Route Matrix and Route Optimization, run locally.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Search along a route</h3>
        <p>Find places inside a route corridor and return trip progress, direction, off-route distance, and the closest rejoin point — over a route you computed or one you already had.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Live traffic, from a feed you choose</h3>
        <p>A pluggable provider contract: a municipal feed, your own telemetry, or the PulseMesh peer-to-peer channel. When it is absent the router degrades to the static metric and still answers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Inline place details</h3>
        <p>Return configured OSM details and geometry with search results, avoiding a separately billed details request.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Your renderer and infrastructure</h3>
        <p>Start with the free public index, or pair Rangefind with MapLibre, Leaflet, or OpenLayers and host your own immutable index on any range-capable CDN.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section maps-scope">
    <span class="eyebrow">HONEST COMPATIBILITY</span>
    <h2>Google Maps Platform versus Rangefind OSM</h2>
    <p>
      Use Rangefind for the OSM-backed search, geocoding, and routing surface.
      Keep a specialist provider only for capabilities that require licensed
      or proprietary data.
    </p>
    <div class="maps-table-wrap">
      <table class="maps-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Capability</th><th>Rangefind replacement</th><th>Important boundary</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Places text search</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — names, brands, categories, addresses, localities</td><td>Quality follows the indexed OSM data</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Place autocomplete</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — weighted, multilingual, typo-aware suggestions</td><td>No billing session token is needed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Nearby and viewport search</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — radius, distance, bounds, typed constraints</td><td>Popularity is a static prominence signal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Forward/reverse geocoding</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — including OSM address interpolation</td><td>Address completeness varies by region</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Place details and geometry</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — configured OSM fields returned inline</td><td>No Google reviews, ratings, or photos</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Map rendering</td><td>Use MapLibre, Leaflet, OpenLayers, or another renderer</td><td>Rangefind does not supply basemap tiles</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Directions and ETA</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — car, bike, and foot routes with sign-accurate steps</td><td>No tolls or proprietary road data</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Distance matrix and stop ordering</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — <code>matrix()</code> and <code>itinerary()</code></td><td>One vehicle; no capacities, time windows, or fleet assignment</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Live traffic</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — through a pluggable provider contract</td><td>You supply the feed or run the mesh; absent data degrades to the static metric</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Journey and shipment tracking</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — PulseMesh threads</td><td>The tracking channel, not a dispatch backend or operator console</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="band maps-cost-band">
    <div class="band__inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">NO SEARCH SAAS · NO PER-REQUEST METER</span>
      <h2>Free software with predictable infrastructure costs</h2>
      <div class="band__cols">
        <div>
          <h3>What is free</h3>
          <p>
            Rangefind is MIT licensed. There is no Rangefind account, API key,
            request quota, monthly search plan, or charge for place details.
            The browser can query the public <code>osm.rangefind.dev</code>
            index or files hosted under your control.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>What can still cost money</h3>
          <p>
            OSM extraction and index builds need compute, and production
            storage, CDN traffic, and map tiles may have provider costs. The
            difference is that these are cacheable infrastructure costs—not a
            proprietary fee attached to every user query.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

