  <section class="section section--last maps-faq">
    <span class="eyebrow">COMMON MIGRATION QUESTIONS</span>
    <h2>Is Rangefind the right Google Maps alternative?</h2>
    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Can I use it without an API key?</h3>
        <p>Yes. The runtime reads a static index directly from your origin or CDN. There is no Rangefind account or query credential.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Can it replace Google Places API?</h3>
        <p>For OSM-backed text, nearby, autocomplete, details, constraints, and geocoding use cases, yes. Proprietary ratings, reviews, photos, and live occupancy need another source.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Does it provide map tiles or directions?</h3>
        <p>Directions yes — routes, ETAs, matrices, and itineraries come from a static route graph read the same way. Tiles no: use a map renderer and tile source for display.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Does “free” mean zero infrastructure cost?</h3>
        <p>No. The software and query layer have no fee, but you still own build compute, storage, CDN traffic, and any tile-provider costs.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
